  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In October 2020, the Government of Eswatini adopted recommendations from a situational analysis of the labour market information system with a view to address existing weaknesses in the skills anticipation component of the system. Working in consultation with the Social Partners, the Government used the recommendations of the situation analysis to develop a national action plan that will guide the process of integrating and strengthening the skills anticipation elements of the National Labour Market Information System. A national Task Team drafted and finalized the action plan after an inter-departmental and inter-ministerial consultative process and submitted it to the Ministry of Labour. With support from the European Union, the ILO supported the analysis of the country&apos;s policies, capacities, institutional arrangements and social dialogue mechanisms for labour market information system with the view to identify weaknesses and to provide concrete recommendations for improving the systems. This gender-responsive situation analysis was conducted from June - October 2020. The ILO further supported hosting of a stakeholder workshop to review and validate the findings and recommendations of the report in October 2020.

The ILO also facilitated the training of six labour market and skills development experts from the Ministry of Labour, Statistics Office and Ministry of Education who underwent training on how to position skills anticipation in the country&apos;s broader labour market policy framework with a focus on how to anticipate skills for sectors that can foster economic diversification and employment creation, and to strengthen their capacities on how to establish national level skills anticipation frameworks.  

The ILO provided the Ministry of Labour and the national task team on Skills anticipation with technical guidance on the development of a national action plan that will guide the steps of strengthening the skills component of the Eswatini Labour Market information system.</narrative>
   </description>
